P3 = """<div class="page"><div><div class="rhead"><span>The Household Botanical Blueprint</span><span>Section 02 &bull; Label &amp; Home Audits</span></div><div class="kicker">Part I &bull; Turn The Bottle Around</div><h2>The 76.8% Mineral Oil Audit &amp; Three Home Tests</h2><p>Walk into your bathroom cabinet right now, pick up a commercial hair or body oil, and read the back panel. On one of India&rsquo;s most trusted commercial almond hair oils, <strong>light liquid paraffin</strong>&mdash;petroleum-derived mineral oil&mdash;accounts for <strong>76.8% of the bottle</strong>, paired with synthetic fragrance and industrial dyes behind traditional Ayurvedic imagery.</p><div class="wcard rt"><div class="ctitle" style="color:#9E3B2B">The Five-Second Back-Label Red Flag Checklist</div><div class="g2" style="margin:4px 0 0"><ul style="margin-bottom:0"><li><strong>Paraffinum Liquidum / Mineral Oil:</strong> Any petroleum distillate listed in the top three ingredients.</li><li><strong>&ldquo;Fragrance&rdquo; or &ldquo;Parfum&rdquo;:</strong> Undisclosed synthetic aroma chemicals and phthalate fixatives.</li></ul><ul style="margin-bottom:0"><li><strong>Missing Binomial Latin Name:</strong> Genuine essential oils always state the exact species (e.g., <em>Lavandula angustifolia</em>).</li><li><strong>&ldquo;Fragrance Oil&rdquo; / &ldquo;Aroma Oil&rdquo;:</strong> Perfumery solvents containing zero therapeutic steam-distilled plant volatiles.</li></ul></div></div><div class="g3"><div class="wcard gt"><div class="ctitle">Test 1: White Paper Blot</div><p style="font-size:8.9pt;margin-bottom:5px">Place <strong>1 drop</strong> of neat essential oil on unbleached white paper. Wait 15 minutes at room temperature.</p><p style="font-size:8.7pt;margin-bottom:0"><strong>Pass:</strong> Evaporates cleanly with zero grease ring (heavy resins leave a faint tint, never an oily slick).<br><strong>Fail:</strong> Persistent greasy halo = carrier/mineral dilution.</p></div><div class="wcard gt"><div class="ctitle">Test 2: Cold-Water Drop</div><p style="font-size:8.9pt;margin-bottom:5px">Drop <strong>1 drop</strong> into a clear glass of room-temperature water and observe from the side.</p><p style="font-size:8.7pt;margin-bottom:0"><strong>Pass:</strong> Forms cohesive micro-beads on the surface (or sinks cleanly for dense oils like Wintergreen/Clove).<br><strong>Fail:</strong> Turns milky immediately = synthetic emulsifiers.</p></div><div class="wcard gt"><div class="ctitle">Test 3: Finger-Rub Audit</div><p style="font-size:8.9pt;margin-bottom:5px">Rub <strong>1 drop</strong> between thumb and index finger for 10 seconds.</p><p style="font-size:8.7pt;margin-bottom:0"><strong>Pass:</strong> Feels crisp, absorbs rapidly, and leaves skin velvety without slick residue.<br><strong>Fail:</strong> Feels slippery or waxy = paraffin or silicone filler.</p></div></div><div class="wcard gl" style="margin-bottom:0"><div class="ctitle">Why Companies Use Paraffin Instead of Plant Oils</div><p style="margin-bottom:0">Cosmetic mineral oil is refined to be non-toxic and virtually incapable of going rancid. Manufacturers favor it because it costs a fraction of cold-pressed botanicals and sits on warehouse shelves for years without spoiling&mdash;forming an occlusive plastic-like film over skin and hair rather than delivering living plant fatty acids.</p></div></div><div class="rfoot"><span>Kalyani Botanical Archive &bull; kalyanima.in</span><span>Page 3</span></div></div>"""
P4 = f"""<div class="page"><div><div class="rhead"><span>The Household Botanical Blueprint</span><span>Section 03 &bull; Glass-Base Proof</span></div><div class="kicker">Part II &bull; Inspect The Bottom Of The Glass</div><h2>Batch Codes, Expiration Stamps &amp; GC/MS Chemistry</h2><p>Front labels are written by marketing departments. The bottom of the amber glass bottle reveals whether a producer actually tests every harvest batch and stands behind its shelf life.</p><div class="g2"><div class="wcard rt" style="text-align:center">{blk_v}<h3 style="color:#9E3B2B">Unverified Mass-Market Bottle</h3><p style="font-size:8.9pt;text-align:left;margin-bottom:0">Smooth, blank glass base with <strong>no lot code</strong> and <strong>no expiration date</strong>. Synthetic fragrance compounds and inert mineral oil carriers do not oxidize like real plant volatiles, so mass-market brands rarely stamp a batch trace or expiry date.</p></div><div class="wcard gt" style="text-align:center">{ver_v}<h3 style="color:#102217">Verified Single-Origin Standard</h3><p style="font-size:8.9pt;text-align:left;margin-bottom:0">Features a dot-matrix <strong>batch/lot number</strong> (e.g., <em>260636A</em>) linked to independent third-party GC/MS lab testing, paired with a printed <strong>expiration date</strong> confirming active, living botanical compounds.</p></div></div><div class="wcard gl"><div class="ctitle">How to Read a Third-Party GC/MS Lab Report in 60 Seconds</div><p style="font-size:9.1pt">Gas Chromatography&ndash;Mass Spectrometry (GC/MS) separates an essential oil into its individual naturally occurring molecules and measures each one by percentage. Check three markers:</p><ol style="margin-bottom:0"><li><strong>Batch ID Match:</strong> Confirm the report header matches the exact lot code stamped on the bottom of your bottle.</li><li><strong>Primary Biomarker Range:</strong> Verify key natural constituents fall within true botanical norms (e.g., 35&ndash;45% 1,8-cineole in Rosemary; 25&ndash;38% linalool and 25&ndash;45% linalyl acetate in True Lavender).</li><li><strong>Zero Synthetic Markers:</strong> Confirm the report explicitly rules out synthetic diluents (diethyl phthalate, dipropylene glycol, triethyl citrate) and foreign stereoisomers.</li></ol></div><div class="wcard grl" style="margin-bottom:0"><div class="ctitle">Why Amber or Cobalt Glass &amp; Orifice Reducers Matter</div><p style="margin-bottom:0">Ultraviolet light accelerates oxidation of delicate monoterpenes (especially in citrus and pine-needle oils). Always store pure extracts in dark amber or cobalt glass with an integrated orifice reducer dropper, tightly capped away from bathroom steam and direct sunlight.</p></div></div><div class="rfoot"><span>Kalyani Botanical Archive &bull; kalyanima.in</span><span>Page 4</span></div></div>"""
P5 = """<div class="page"><div><div class="rhead"><span>The Household Botanical Blueprint</span><span>Section 04 &bull; Carrier Lipid Chemistry</span></div><div class="kicker">Part III &bull; The Carrier Foundation</div><h2>Cold-Pressed Carrier Oils: Choosing the Right Base</h2><p>Pure steam-distilled essential oils are 50 to 100 times more concentrated than the whole plant. Applying them &ldquo;neat&rdquo; (undiluted) wastes volatile compounds through rapid evaporation and risks skin sensitization. Pairing them with a pure, cold-pressed lipid carrier slows evaporation, carries active molecules across the stratum corneum, and nourishes skin directly.</p><table><thead><tr><th>Carrier Oil</th><th>Dominant Fatty Acid</th><th>Absorption &amp; Feel</th><th>Comedogenic (0&ndash;5)</th><th>Best Household Pairing</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Fractionated Coconut (FCO)</strong><br><em>Cocos nucifera</em></td><td>Caprylic &amp; Capric Triglycerides (MCT)</td><td>Featherlight, non-greasy, odorless, indefinite shelf life</td><td><strong>1 &ndash; 2</strong> (Low)</td><td>Daily roller bottles, pulse-point blends, quick chest &amp; joint rubs.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Cold-Pressed Jojoba</strong><br><em>Simmondsia chinensis</em></td><td>Liquid Wax Esters (Eicosenoic Acid)</td><td>Mirrors human sebum; balanced, silky, non-tacky</td><td><strong>2</strong> (Low)</td><td>Facial night serums (Frankincense / Lavender) &amp; sensitive skin.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Sweet Almond Oil</strong><br><em>Prunus amygdalus dulcis</em></td><td>Oleic (62&ndash;76%) &amp; Linoleic (20&ndash;30%)</td><td>Rich glide, medium absorption, deeply conditioning</td><td><strong>2</strong> (Low)</td><td>Evening foot-sole wind-down massage, dry body skin, family rubs.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Traditional Sesame Oil</strong><br><em>Sesamum indicum</em></td><td>Linoleic (41%) &amp; Oleic (39%) + Sesamol</td><td>Warming, dense, traditional Ayurvedic <em>Abhyanga</em> base</td><td><strong>1 &ndash; 3</strong> (Mod.)</td><td>Warm Sunday <em>champi</em> scalp infusions &amp; winter joint massage.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Virgin Coconut Oil</strong><br><em>Cocos nucifera</em></td><td>Lauric Acid (~48%) &amp; Myristic Acid</td><td>Rich, cooling, solidifies below 24&deg;C (76&deg;F)</td><td><strong>4</strong> (Hair/Body)</td><td>Deep hair shaft conditioning masks &amp; cooling summer scalp oil.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class="g2" style="margin-top:8px"><div class="wcard gl" style="margin-bottom:0"><div class="ctitle">How to Audit Your Carrier Oil Too</div><p style="font-size:9pt;margin-bottom:0">A pure essential oil loses its integrity if diluted into a paraffin-cut carrier. Ensure your carrier bottle states <strong>100% Pure Cold-Pressed</strong> (or Steam-Distilled Fractionated Coconut Oil) with a single botanical ingredient and zero mineral oil.</p></div><div class="wcard grl" style="margin-bottom:0"><div class="ctitle">Standard Measurement Conversion</div><p style="font-size:9pt;margin-bottom:0"><strong>1 Teaspoon (5 mL)</strong> &asymp; 100 drops carrier<br><strong>1 Tablespoon (15 mL)</strong> &asymp; 300 drops carrier<br><strong>10 mL Roller Bottle</strong> &asymp; 200 drops carrier (4 drops EO = 2% dilution).</p></div></div></div><div class="rfoot"><span>Kalyani Botanical Archive &bull; kalyanima.in</span><span>Page 5</span></div></div>"""
